---
import { ISSUES_URL, LICENSE_URL, SUPPORT_EMAIL } from "../config";

// Plain answers to what people ask first. Answers are trusted HTML (for
// <code>); every one must stay true of the current release.
const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is it open source?",
    a: "It is source-available under FSL-1.1-MIT. You can read, modify and redistribute it for anything except a competing commercial product, and each release becomes MIT two years after it ships.",
  },
  {
    q: "Which platforms does it run on?",
    a: "macOS only for now: one universal build for Apple Silicon and Intel, signed and notarized. Linux and Windows builds aren't available yet.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need the jj CLI?",
    a: "No. Jiji embeds jj-lib 0.45, so it reads and writes repositories and config the way jj 0.45 does. Keep <code>jj</code> installed if you like a terminal; both work on the same repository at once. Fetch and push use Git, so <code>git</code> must be on your <code>PATH</code>.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use it on a Git repository?",
    a: "Yes. Opening a plain Git repository offers to set jj up in it, like <code>jj git init --colocate</code>: a <code>.jj</code> folder appears next to <code>.git</code>, your branches become bookmarks, and Git keeps working.",
  },
  {
    q: "How is it different from gg, jjui or VisualJJ?",
    a: "They are good front ends for jj: gg is a desktop app, jjui and lazyjj run in a terminal, VisualJJ and Jujutsu Kaizen live in VS Code. Jiji is a diff-first desktop workbench with stacked-PR publishing, landing and a CLI built in.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is it stable?",
    a: "It is alpha. It is meant for people who know jj well enough to sanity-check what a GUI does. Everything Jiji changes goes through jj's operation log, so <code>jj undo</code> and <code>jj op restore</code> can take any of it back.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do updates work?",
    a: "Jiji checks GitHub Releases, downloads a new version in the background, and offers to restart. It never restarts on its own.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do licenses work?",
    a: "A one-time purchase through Polar. Solo activates one device and Personal up to five. The key is checked with Polar at most once a day and keeps working offline.",
  },
];
---

<section class="band" id="faq">
  <div class="container faq">
    <div class="band-head">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Something else? <a class="link" href={ISSUES_URL}>Open an issue</a> or write to
        <a class="link" href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <dl>
      {
        faqs.map((f) => (
          <div class="item">
            <dt>{f.q}</dt>
            <dd set:html={f.a} />
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p class="meta">
      The full terms are in the <a class="link" href={LICENSE_URL}>license</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  dl {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 0 clamp(20px, 4vw, 48px);
  }

  .item {
    padding: var(--sp-4) 0 var(--sp-5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--clr-border-2);
  }

  dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  dd {
    margin-top: var(--sp-2);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--clr-text-2);
  }

  .meta {
    margin-top: var(--sp-5);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    dl {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
